Every conversation pancake has on the wire is built from a small, fixed vocabulary,
defined by the
[OCapN specifications](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn/tree/main/draft-specifications):
the CapTP messages, the descriptors they carry, and the locators that say where a peer
lives. This page is the one-glance map: what each part is for, in a sentence. For
the why behind them, [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) tells the story and the
[protocol diagrams](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/README.md)
draw the flows.

The list is not hand-kept: a build check pins every entry on this page to the
labels the Syrup codec reads and writes. If the protocol surface grows a label
or drops one, this page moves with it or the gate goes red.

## Operations

An operation (`op:...`) is a message that does something in the session: it opens the
session, makes a call, settles a promise, or tears the whole thing down.

| Message | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `op:start-session` | Opens a session. Each side announces its location and the keys that bind the connection. |
| `op:deliver` | Calls a method on a reference and registers an answer, so the caller holds a promise the instant the call goes out. |
| `op:resolve` | Settles a promise to its final value, or to a break if the call failed. |
| `op:listen` | Subscribes to a promise's resolution, so a waiter is told the answer the moment it lands. |
| `op:abort` | Ends the session with a typed reason. The connection is finished. |
| `op:get` | Projects a named field off an answer without waiting for the whole result to settle. |
| `op:index` | Projects a positional element off an answer. |
| `op:untag` | Unwraps a tagged value projected off an answer. |
| `op:gc-exports` | Tells the peer that some exported references are no longer held, so it can drop them. |
| `op:gc-answers` | Tells the peer that some answers are no longer needed, so it can release them. |

## Reference descriptors

A descriptor (`desc:...`) is not a message on its own. It is how a reference or a
handoff rides *inside* a message, naming what a slot in the conversation points at.

| Descriptor | What it names |
| --- | --- |
| `desc:export` | A reference to an object the sender exports for the other side to call. |
| `desc:answer` | A reference to a pending answer from an earlier `op:deliver`, the target of promise pipelining. |
| `desc:import-object` | The receiver's side of a `desc:export`: the same object, as the importer sees it. |
| `desc:import-promise` | The receiver's side of a promised reference, settled later by an `op:resolve`. |
| `desc:handoff-give` | The gifter's half of a three-party introduction: "go meet this peer, on my word." |
| `desc:handoff-receive` | The recipient's half of that introduction, redeemed with the third party. |
| `desc:sig-envelope` | A signed wrapper that makes a handoff unforgeable as it crosses between the three parties. |

## Locators

A locator (`ocapn-...`) is not a message either, and not a reference. It is an
*address*: where a peer lives, and, for a sturdyref, which object to ask it for. A
locator rides inside `op:start-session` as the location each side announces, and
travels as an argument when one peer hands another a long-lived reference to enliven
later. The `ocapn://` URI you paste into a config is one of these records, in text.

| Locator | What it addresses |
| --- | --- |
| `ocapn-peer` | A peer's location: its transport (`tcp`, `iroh`), the designator to reach it at, and any connection hints. An `ocapn://<designator>.<transport>` URI is this record. |
| `ocapn-sturdyref` | A peer location plus a swiss number: a restorable, long-lived reference to one object, the thing an `ocapn://…/s/<swiss>` URI names. |

If a name here is new, start with [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')), which
introduces capabilities, promises, and the three-party handoff before any of these
labels appear. The [Transports]($link.page('transports')) page covers the netlayers
a locator can name.
